    2595	### D17 — Discrete Morse theory on the divisibility poset: critical-cell complexity of the prime indicator
    2596	
    2597	**Question:** Forman's discrete Morse theory (Forman 1998 *Adv. Math.*
    2598	134; Forman 2002 *Sém. Lothar. Combin.* 48) attaches to any finite
    2599	regular CW complex `K` a discrete *Morse matching* `mu` on its face
    2600	poset such that the *critical cells* `Crit(K, mu)` form a sub-complex
    2601	with the same homology as `K` but typically far fewer cells. Build the
    2602	*divisibility order complex* `Delta(N) := order complex of (N_{<= N}, |)`
    2603	and assign a discrete Morse function `f(n) = log n` (or
    2604	`f([a_0 < a_1 < ... < a_k]) = k`). Question: what is the asymptotic
    2605	size `|Crit(Delta(N), mu_*)|` for the OPTIMAL Morse matching as
    2606	`N -> infty`? Does it scale as `c * pi(N)` (capturing primality count
    2607	asymptotically), as `O(log N)^c` (radical compression), or as
    2608	`Theta(N)` (no compression)? If `|Crit(Delta(N))| = O(polylog N)`,
    2609	the prime indicator IS topologically compressible at the cell-counting
    2610	level — first known polylog representation of an arithmetic structure
    2611	on the divisibility lattice.
    2612	
    2613	**Why frontier:** **no published work computes the discrete-Morse
    2614	critical-cell complexity of the divisibility order complex.**
    2615	Bjorner-Wachs 2005 (*Trans. AMS* 357) studied poset homotopy types of
    2616	related arithmetic posets but never the Morse-critical-cell count.
    2617	The question is a clean combinatorial extremal problem with a
    2618	direct algorithmic translation: an explicit optimal Morse matching of
    2619	size `O(N - polylog(N))` would mean `pi(N)` is recoverable from the
    2620	critical-cell list at polylog cost. Furthermore, Benedetti-Lutz 2014
    2621	(*Exp. Math.* 23) gave a polynomial-time greedy algorithm `random
    2622	discrete Morse` that achieves near-optimal matchings on combinatorial
    2623	complexes; running it on `Delta(N)` at `N` up to `~10^4` is a single-
    2624	session experiment.
